{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The claimed exact obstruction characterization is incomplete. Locally stable mixed configurations are exactly those where crossing edges form a matching; after deleting crossing edges, each monochromatic component only needs minimum degree 2, not necessarily an induced cycle or an isolated single-edge cut. A concrete omitted obstruction is a 12-vertex 3-regular graph with two disjoint crossing edges a-b and c-d (a,c red; b,d blue), where the red side is the 4-cycle a1-a2-c1-c2-a1 with a attached to a1,a2 and c attached to c1,c2, and the blue side is built analogously. Every vertex has exactly one crossing edge and two same-color edges, so it is locally stable, but it is neither an induced cycle with all third edges crossing nor a single-edge cut. This omission invalidates the enumeration and the Poisson constant c derived from it.","counterexample":["Two disjoint crossing edges a-b and c-d; red component: 4-cycle a1-a2-c1-c2-a1 with a attached to a1,a2 and c attached to c1,c2; blue component analogous. All vertices have degree 3 and at most one crossing edge, so stable, but not an induced cycle or single-edge cut."],"target":"de3a96ef284a2940a94638f9360ed6f1d5ffb14b3775061950be8897601d5051"}]}